.TH git-push-all "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Git Command"
.SH NAME
git-push-all \- push the current branch to every remote that allows pushing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git push-all
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBgit push-all\fR command pushes the current branch to each remote of the repository with
\fBgit push\fR \fIREMOTE BRANCH\fR, one after the other, printing the remote before each push.
.PP
A remote whose push URL is \fBDISABLED\fR (or disabled, no_push, no-push) is skipped. That is how
\fBsmi-project-prepare\fR(1) protects the template remote \fBupstream\fR:
.PP
.B git remote set-url --push upstream DISABLED
.PP
When one push fails, the others are still tried and the command exits with status 1. It fails at once
when no branch is checked out (detached HEAD).
.PP
Git runs it as a subcommand because the executable \fBgit-push-all\fR is in the \fBPATH\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.B git push-all
.PP
In a project made by \fBsmi-project-prepare\fR(1), on master, it prints:
.PP
.nf
origin: git push origin master
\&...
upstream: push disabled, skipped
.fi
